pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberInsultingMoneyCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Never insult money or gamble unless you want to banish it"@en, "Geld nie beleidigen oder verspielen, es sei denn, man will es bannen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Some neophytes imagine they can conjure wealth by burning bank notes to the gods. Never insult money or blaspheme it by gambling, unless you want to banish it."@en ;
    skos:note "The caution carries its own operation: insulting money or gambling banishes it. Entered here and not as a practice of its own, since the work gives it only as the reverse of the advice."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: burning bank notes to the gods as a conjuration of wealth is named as a neophyte's error, an observable act; the failure is put on the operator."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicPsyberInvestNotSacrifice ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 7 Magick IV, Sacrifice, commentary, p. 21; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberMoneyAsGod a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Money as a vast intelligent organism living in nearly every brain"@en, "Geld als riesiger intelligenter Organismus, der in fast jedem Gehirn lebt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Money acts as a vast, intelligent organism which lives by occupying part of the brain of nearly everyone on this planet, and seems far more awake at this moment than many gods; it is to be treated as a major God."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 7 Magick IV, Sacrifice, commentary, p. 21; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberHomeopathyByMagick a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Homeopathy works by magick, the parapsychological effect of operator intent"@en, "Homöopathie wirkt durch Magick, den parapsychologischen Effekt der Absicht des Behandelnden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Paradox: if homeopaths knew how homeopathy actually worked, it wouldn't work. Homeopathy works by magick, the parapsychological effect of operator intent; its erroneous theories arose from the early empirical practices of immunisation and an ignorance of Avogadro's number, and an understanding of acquired immunity would have prevented them. Homeopathy has let magick back into medicine at the price of demanding belief in a completely wrong theory by its practitioners."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 8 Magick V, Magickal Medicine, text, p. 22; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 8 Magick V, Magickal Medicine, commentary, p. 23; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberWrongTheoryBelief a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The practitioner's belief in the homeopathic theory"@en, "Der Glaube des Behandelnden an die homöopathische Theorie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The homeopath's belief in a theory the work calls bogus."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 8 Magick V, Magickal Medicine, text, p. 22; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberBeliefBeforeHomeopathy a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Homeopaths have to believe in bogus theories to achieve real effects"@en, "Homöopathen müssen an falsche Theorien glauben, um wirkliche Wirkungen zu erzielen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Homeopaths have to believe in bogus theories to achieve real effects; if they knew how homeopathy actually worked, it would not work."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberHomeopathyByMagick ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberWrongTheoryBelief ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If homeopaths knew how homeopathy actually worked, it wouldn't work." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 8 Magick V, Magickal Medicine, text, p. 22; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsyberIatrogenicHomeopathy a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Iatrogenic homeopathy: antidoting a target with a potentization of itself"@en, "Iatrogene Homöopathie: ein Ziel mit einer Potenzierung seiner selbst antidotieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "A spell of black magick: the sorcerer antidotes the target with a potentization of itself. The author adds that perhaps he should not have mentioned it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 8 Magick V, Magickal Medicine, commentary, p. 23; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberIatrogenicHomeopathyYieldsHarm a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Iatrogenic homeopathy apparently works well in black magick"@en, "Iatrogene Homöopathie wirkt anscheinend gut in schwarzer Magick"@de ;
    skos:definition "Iatrogenic homeopathy apparently works well in black magick: the target is antidoted with a potentization of itself."@en ;
    skos:note "The work says apparently and names no case."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsyberIatrogenicHomeopathy ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberHarmToAnEnemy ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 8 Magick V, Magickal Medicine, commentary, p. 23; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberFourthEquation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The fourth equation of magick: Pe = P + (1 - P)L"@en, "Die vierte Gleichung der Magick: Pe = P + (1 - P)L"@de ;
    skos:definition "Pe = P + (1 - P)L, all factors on a scale of 0-1, where Pe is the probability of accomplishing something by the use of an eidolon, P the probability of the event occurring by chance alone, and L the quality of the magical link; the first three equations are those of Liber Kaos."@en ;
    skos:note "The text layer reads the last factor as U; the legend names it L. The equation sets the chance rate P inside the expected success rate, which makes an eidolon's result readable only against a stated P."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 9 Magick VI, The Fourth Equation of Magick, Eidolonics, text, p. 24; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsyberEidolonEvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Eidolon Evocation: a material basis carried on the body and its astral image held to clear hallucination"@en, "Die Eidolon-Evokation: eine am Körper getragene materielle Grundlage und ihr astrales Bild bis zur klaren Halluzination gehalten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Let the magickian fashion a material basis for a general purpose cybermorph, sparing no expense or skill, and physically carry it at all times close to the body; mentally carry its astral image to the point of clear hallucination during any spare waking moment and if possible while dreaming; and by profound effort of ritual, phantasy and imagination treat the eidolon's material and astral forms as sigils for a named semi-autonomous sentience."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: the astral image held to the point of clear hallucination, announced beforehand as the mark that the mental carrying is sufficient; its base rate under such sustained visualization is unknown."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 9 Magick VI, The Fourth Equation of Magick, Eidolonics, commentary, p. 25; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberReusableServitor a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A reusable multi-task servitor for which the first equation's factors take unit value"@en, "Ein wiederverwendbarer Diener für viele Aufgaben, für den die Faktoren der ersten Gleichung den Wert eins annehmen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A reusable multi-task servitor, the eidolon cybermorph, for which the factors of the first equation take an optimum unitary value, so that only the magickal link awaits solution in any particular spell of enchantment or divination directed through it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 9 Magick VI, The Fourth Equation of Magick, Eidolonics, commentary, p. 25; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberEidolonEvocationYieldsServitor a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The Eidolon Evocation creates a reusable multi-task servitor"@en, "Die Eidolon-Evokation schafft einen wiederverwendbaren Diener für viele Aufgaben"@de ;
    skos:definition "By such conjurations the magickian will create a reusable multi-task servitor, leaving only the magickal link to solve; the master will usually prefer the speed and efficiency an eidolon cybermorph offers."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsyberEidolonEvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberReusableServitor ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 9 Magick VI, The Fourth Equation of Magick, Eidolonics, commentary, p. 25; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberManyConjurations a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The many and varied conjurations of the art"@en, "Die vielen und vielfältigen Beschwörungen der Kunst"@de ;
    skos:definition "The many and varied conjurations of the art and science the neophyte must learn."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 9 Magick VI, The Fourth Equation of Magick, Eidolonics, commentary, p. 25; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberConjurationsBeforeEidolon a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The neophyte must learn the many conjurations; the master prefers the eidolon"@en, "Der Neuling muss die vielen Beschwörungen lernen; der Meister bevorzugt das Eidolon"@de ;
    skos:definition "Although the neophyte must learn the many and varied conjurations of the art and science, the master will usually prefer the greater speed and efficiency of an eidolon cybermorph."@en ;
    skos:note "The sentence orders neophyte and master and states no consequence of using an eidolon without the conjurations; entered at prescribed order."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicPsyberEidolonEvocation ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberManyConjurations ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 9 Magick VI, The Fourth Equation of Magick, Eidolonics, commentary, p. 25; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberFifthEquation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The fifth equation of magick: zero separation in six-dimensional psispacetime"@en, "Die fünfte Gleichung der Magick: Abstand null in der sechsdimensionalen Psiraumzeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The fifth equation gives the psispacetime separation between events in a six-dimensional Pythagorean form, with s the spatial separation, t the separation in ordinary time, a and b the separations in two dimensions of imaginary time, and c lightspeed; set to zero it simplifies to s^2 - (ct)^2 + (ca)^2 + (cb)^2 = 0. Non-local exchange of information can occur when the separation is zero; quantum non-locality is the limited case a = b = 0, information passing instantaneously along lightcones, and magick the more general case. Space and time are modelled with the same dimensionality of three; one cannot see the other two dimensions of time, nor even the first."@en ;
    skos:note "The text layer garbles the full form of the equation and the condition for terrestrial distances; the simplified form of the commentary is given as read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 10 Magick VII, The Fifth Equation of Magick, text, p. 26; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 10 Magick VII, The Fifth Equation of Magick, commentary, p. 27; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberNullPathEnchantment a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Null path enchantments: spells act on a circle of probabilities in imaginary time"@en, "Nullpfad-Verzauberungen: Zauber wirken auf einen Kreis von Wahrscheinlichkeiten in imaginärer Zeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Figure 1 shows three dimensions of time with a circle of unit radius in the plane ab. Enchantments launched at t0 act on the circle of probabilities at t1 and may appear as changed conditions when the magickian arrives there; they land in the future at twice the radius of the circle they interact with. Retroactive enchantments act on a circle at t-1, apparently altering past properties, which feed back to the present; as only the present instant is observable, any debate about what really occurred becomes academic. The probability circle grows with time by a factor of pi t. An observer cannot observe or interact with the parallel universes of the circle at t0; they may exist but may as well not."@en ;
    skos:note "Figure 1 is in the layer only as its caption."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 11 Magick VIII, Null Path Enchantments, text, p. 28; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 11 Magick VIII, Null Path Enchantments, commentary, p. 29; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberRetroactiveSelfConsistencyCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "A retroactive enchantment cannot alter the past so as to prevent itself"@en, "Eine rückwirkende Verzauberung kann die Vergangenheit nicht so ändern, dass sie sich selbst verhindert"@de ;
    skos:definition "Retroactive enchantments can apparently alter past properties only so long as these do not have the effect of preventing the enchantment."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicRetroactiveEnchantment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 11 Magick VIII, Null Path Enchantments, text, p. 28; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberNullPathDivination a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Null path divinations: scrying succeeds only by identifying a dominant probability"@en, "Nullpfad-Divinationen: Schauen gelingt nur durch Erkennen einer vorherrschenden Wahrscheinlichkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Divinations performed at t0 to scry events at t2 or t-2 can only succeed if the magickian can identify a dominant probability amongst the plethora of information in either circle of imaginary time (figure 2). One can only scry the imaginary past of an event at t2, not the event itself."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: only divinations which reveal unexpectedly high probabilities tend to count as successes. The mark is defined against expectation, but the work gives no record of what was expected before the divination."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 12 Magick IX, Null Path Divinations, text, p. 30; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberLookingDistortsCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Looking in divination distorts the probability of what is looked at"@en, "Das Schauen in der Divination verzerrt die Wahrscheinlichkeit des Geschauten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The very act of looking in divination can tend to distort the probability of what you look at; divination can thus act as enchantment, beyond the effect of self-fulfilling prophecy in the ordinary sense."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberNullPathDivination ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 12 Magick IX, Null Path Divinations, commentary, p. 31; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberDivinationDifficultyCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Divination grows harder with time, and nobody divines consistently"@en, "Divination wird mit der Zeit schwerer, und niemand weissagt beständig"@de ;
    skos:definition "Divination becomes progressively more difficult with time as the circles of probability in imaginary time enlarge. The author has never met anyone who could consistently divine accurately, though a huge market exists for those who divine poorly or merely pretend to; the market for those who can enchant reasonably well hardly exists, because those who can use it to their own advantage, and the same may apply to divination but the author doubts it on account of the technical difficulties."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: no one met by the author could divine consistently and accurately; the failure is put on the structure of imaginary time and not on the operator, and the same chapter explains the absence of good enchanters from the market so that their absence cannot count against enchantment."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberNullPathDivination ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 12 Magick IX, Null Path Divinations, commentary, p. 31; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsyberLostObjectExperiment a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The lost-object experiment: tricking the subconscious into having put the object where one wants to find it"@en, "Das Experiment mit dem verlorenen Gegenstand: das Unterbewusste glauben machen, man habe ihn dorthin gelegt, wo man ihn finden will"@de ;
    skos:definition "Wait until something is lost. Rather than search further, try to trick the subconscious into believing the object was actually put in a particular place where you want to find it; a place already searched may be used, but then the subconscious recollection of having searched it must be made as hazy as possible. Then, while occupying the conscious mind with some powerful distraction, anger working well, go to the chosen location and retrieve the object. The work offers it to magickians who have difficulty summoning the belief that makes retroactive enchantment work on the macroscopic scale, despite the ease with which the effect is said to manifest in delayed-choice quantum optics; the coordination of will and perception is compared to catching a ball, both working best when automatic."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: the object found at the location chosen. The work names no base rate of finding a lost object in a place one wants to look, and its own instruction to make the memory of an earlier search hazy removes the record against which a find would be measured."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 13 Magick X, Retroactive Enchantment, text, p. 32; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberRetrievingLostObjects a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Retrieving a lost object from the place one chose by retroactive enchantment"@en, "Einen verlorenen Gegenstand am selbst gewählten Ort durch rückwirkende Verzauberung wiederfinden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Finding a lost object at a place chosen in advance, by altering its imaginary-time past."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 13 Magick X, Retroactive Enchantment, text, p. 32; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberLostObjectYieldsRetrieval a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The lost-object experiment retrieves the object at the chosen location"@en, "Das Experiment holt den Gegenstand am gewählten Ort zurück"@de ;
    skos:definition "Going to the chosen location with the conscious mind distracted, one retrieves the lost object there."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsyberLostObjectExperiment ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberRetrievingLostObjects ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 13 Magick X, Retroactive Enchantment, text, p. 32; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberSuspendedDeliberation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Suspension of conscious deliberation and memory"@en, "Aussetzen bewusster Überlegung und Erinnerung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The suspension of conscious deliberation and memory, as in catching a ball automatically."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 13 Magick X, Retroactive Enchantment, text, p. 32; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberSuspensionBeforeRetroactiveEnchantment a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Retroactive enchantment depends on suspending conscious deliberation and memory"@en, "Rückwirkende Verzauberung hängt vom Aussetzen bewusster Überlegung und Erinnerung ab"@de ;
    skos:definition "The subtle mental manoeuvres required for retroactive enchantment depend upon suspending conscious deliberation and memory, and will fail if you try thinking consciously about your thoughts."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the operation fails if one thinks consciously about one's thoughts; an internal state only the operator can report, and the failure is put on him."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicPsyberLostObjectExperiment ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberSuspendedDeliberation ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "It will fail if you try thinking consciously about your thoughts." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 13 Magick X, Retroactive Enchantment, text, p. 32; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberRetainingTheAbilityCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "To keep the ability without going mad, seek no alternative explanation of a success"@en, "Um die Fähigkeit ohne Wahnsinn zu behalten, keine andere Erklärung eines Erfolgs suchen"@de ;
    skos:definition "If you ever succeed with such a trick and wish to retain and develop the ability without going mad, do not seek alternative explanations, but accept the belief that the past has some mutability, or better that no real time past exists and the imaginary time past contains probabilities restricted only by the observed conditions of the present moment."@en ;
    skos:note "A self-warning against checking: the work tells the successful operator not to look for the explanation that would count the find as chance, which closes the experiment to its own control."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicPsyberLostObjectExperiment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 13 Magick X, Retroactive Enchantment, text, p. 32; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .


#
# Politics I-VI
#

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberChaocracy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Chaocracy: a legislature selected by lot"@en, "Chaokratie: eine durch Los bestimmte Gesetzgebung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The problem: government must be delegated; nobody who wants political power should have it; de-electable bodies keep power by serving the stasis quo; elected bodies abrogate power to people acting as monarchs. Out of faith in the stochastic principles to which we owe our existence, a legislative body is to be selected by purely random means, paid beyond corruption, half replaced by lot every few years, assisted by a civil service, debating openly and legislating by secret ballot, the outcome of the vote acting as head of state; as we trust lives to randomly selected juries, no less fair method should govern a nation or a planet. Democracy is called the least worst of all systems tried so far, and the question what will succeed it as unaskable as what would succeed monotheism in the fourteenth century. A chaocracy will contain representative proportions of all ages and sexes and free us from the conflict of party ideology with conscience and from casting votes amongst professional liars."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 14 Politics I, Chaocracy, text, p. 34; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 14 Politics I, Chaocracy, commentary, p. 35; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberConspiracyTheory a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Conspiracy theory: conspiracies spiral up from the selfs, but Fuckup rules the outcome"@en, "Verschwörungstheorie: Verschwörungen steigen von den Selbsten auf, doch das Versagen regiert den Ausgang"@de ;
    skos:definition "Nobody has unity of desire; the selfs within the person conspire to fulfil their instinctual agendas, and such conspiracies spiral upward in fractal self-similarity through domestic, social, workplace, national and international scenarios, yielding chaotic outcomes at all levels. Conspiracies exist, but Fuckup mostly rules the outcome. Conspiracy theory, like causality, works only backwards as an explanatory model. Three things feed it: paranoia born of the need for self-importance; the need for enemies; and the desire for belief even in malignant control rather than in pure chance and accident. That we cannot predict much about tomorrow strongly indicates that most explanations of how something happened yesterday have, like history in general, a high bullshit content."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 15 Politics II, Conspiracy Theory, text, p. 36; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberConspiracyPractise a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Conspiracy practise: a conspiracy without internal conspiracies rules its world"@en, "Verschwörungspraxis: eine Verschwörung ohne innere Verschwörungen beherrscht ihre Welt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Any conspiracy lacking internal conspiracies will rule its world; only through absolute loyalty to each other can the few control the many. In practice the power of a conspiracy rises and falls inversely with that of its internal conspiracies; mutual guilt and bribery hold together those whose ideologies command insufficient loyalty, which makes them vulnerable. The simplicity of the two statements belies their applicability at all fractal levels and the virtual impossibility of their achievement."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 16 Politics III, Conspiracy Practise, text, p. 38; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 16 Politics III, Conspiracy Practise, commentary, p. 39; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberBetrayalCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Never join a conspiracy you could possibly betray"@en, "Nie einer Verschwörung beitreten, die man verraten könnte"@de ;
    skos:definition "Never join a conspiracy that you could possibly betray, because if you could, someone else will."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberConspiracyPractise ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 16 Politics III, Conspiracy Practise, commentary, p. 39; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberTetragrammatonOfTheIlluminati a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The Tetragrammaton of the Illuminati: law and chaos against nice and nasty, four programmes"@en, "Das Tetragrammaton der Illuminaten: Gesetz und Chaos gegen nett und gemein, vier Programme"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two axes: law (tradition, order, stasis quo) against chaos (innovation, neophilia), and nice (altruism, inclusive selfishness) against nasty (narrow, exclusive selfishness). The Jehovic programme of lawful nastiness dominates most psychopolitical systems from the nation state down to the selfs within a person, keeping its stasis quo at the expense of surrounding systems; the Christic and Satanic programmes expend themselves against it and each other, and it can use both, saints and criminals. Only the Luciferic programme allows evolution, as the Illuminati know; the dominant Jehovic forces have learned that a limited amount of Luciferic creative chaos gives an edge, and the author aims to give them more than they bargained for. The commentary is headed The Occult Technology of Power."@en ;
    skos:note "The diagram of the four quadrants is lost in the text layer; only its axis labels survive, and which quadrant the Christic, Satanic and Luciferic programmes occupy is not stated in the surviving words."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 17 Politics IV, Tetragrammaton of the Illuminati, text, p. 40; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 17 Politics IV, Tetragrammaton of the Illuminati, commentary, p. 41; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberEqualityOfOpportunity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Liberty, equality, fraternity: only equality of opportunity yields a creative meritocracy"@en, "Freiheit, Gleichheit, Brüderlichkeit: nur Chancengleichheit ergibt eine schöpferische Meritokratie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Fraternity must be discounted once numbers exceed the maximum cohesive pack size of about a hundred and fifty persons, leaving equality at war with liberty: enforced equality destroys liberty, total liberty leads to aristocracy and decay, total equality to oppressive stateism and decay, but equality of opportunity leads to a vibrantly chaotic and creative meritocracy. The commentary attacks creeping stateism and equality of reward and asks whether it falls to students of magick to champion a measured libertarianism. No left-wing party is said to respect liberal economic values, and most centre and right-wing parties merely promote a paternalistic stateism."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 18 Politics V, Revolutions, text, p. 42; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 18 Politics V, Revolutions, commentary, p. 43; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberIlluminatiTactics a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The Illuminati's tactics: amorphous, leaking, attacking at random, enhancing the fuckup factor"@en, "Die Taktik der Illuminaten: gestaltlos, durchlässig, zufällig angreifend, den Versagensfaktor steigernd"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sometime in the twentieth century the Illuminati changed their tactics, conspiring against the stasis quo at all fractal levels simultaneously and dispersing their resources, information and occult technology among innocuous enterprises and enemy camps. Leaking conspiracies are the best vehicles for information and disinformation; enhancing the fuckup factor in an enemy conspiracy subverts it more effectively than direct assault. The order is structured amorphously, does not react to attack, attacks in directions chosen at random and runs away; many members are unknown to each other or unaware of their own membership. The chapter asks whether the reader believes this and wants to make it happen, and closes: immanentize the eschaton any which way you can, shun entropy but exploit chaos to the full. The commentary opens: we can only escape the bloody and ignorant nightmare of history by exploring alternatives which today look frighteningly weird."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 19 Politics VI, The Illuminati, text, p. 44; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 19 Politics VI, The Illuminati, commentary, p. 45; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberIlluminatiNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "The Illuminati, an order conspiring against the stasis quo"@en, "Die Illuminaten, ein Orden, der sich gegen den Status quo verschwört"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Illuminati, named as an order that conspires against the stasis quo, uses the Luciferic programme and has members unaware of their membership."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter asks the reader whether he believes any of it; the naming is entered as the work's, without a claim that it reports an existing body."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Illuminati" ;
    pm:nameRole "order conspiring against the stasis quo at all fractal levels" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 17 Politics IV, Tetragrammaton of the Illuminati, commentary, p. 41; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 19 Politics VI, The Illuminati, text, p. 44; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .


#
# Heresy I-XX
#

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberSpiritualityHeresy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Spirituality as belief in contra-initiative absurdity built on false linguistic equations"@en, "Spiritualität als Glaube an widersinnige Absurdität, gebaut auf falschen sprachlichen Gleichungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magick will not free itself from occultism until the last astrologer is strangled with the guts of the last spiritual master; the heresy chapters are meant to offend everyone. Never discard the negative roots of any equation; always look at the dark side of any enlightenment. Only contra-initiative absurdity commands belief, so a spirituality is best started with something idiotic, denials of death and sex or magick for example. Language alone makes religion possible: as 2 + 2 = 5 would undo mathematics like a virus, the false linguistic equations of spirituality have a like effect on thought."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 20 Heresy I, Spirituality, text, p. 46; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 20 Heresy I, Spirituality, commentary, p. 47; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsyberGoebbelsTechnique a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Goebbels technique: repeating a handy lie until people believe it"@en, "Die Goebbels-Technik: eine handliche Lüge wiederholen, bis die Leute sie glauben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Find a handy lie and keep repeating it loud and long enough until people believe you."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 20 Heresy I, Spirituality, commentary, p. 47; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberSpiritualMastery a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The standing of a spiritual or enlightened master"@en, "Der Rang eines spirituellen oder erleuchteten Meisters"@de ;
    skos:definition "Being taken for a spiritual master or a fully enlightened master."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 20 Heresy I, Spirituality, commentary, p. 47; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 36 Heresy XVII, Machine Enlightenment, commentary, p. 79; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberGoebbelsYieldsMastery a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The Goebbels technique makes one a spiritual master very easily"@en, "Die Goebbels-Technik macht einen sehr leicht zum spirituellen Meister"@de ;
    skos:definition "You can make yourself into a spiritual master very easily using the Goebbels technique."@en ;
    skos:note "Polemic in the form of an instruction; entered as the work states it, since the claim of an attainable standing is the work's."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsyberGoebbelsTechnique ;
