pmp:ChaosMagicPsyberLinguisticEquationExercise a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Exercise: identify the erroneous linguistic equations behind the Supreme Being, Higher Things and Spiritual Values"@en, "Übung: die falschen sprachlichen Gleichungen hinter dem Höchsten Wesen, den Höheren Dingen und den Geistigen Werten finden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Identify the erroneous linguistic equations which create the ideas of the Supreme Being, Higher Things and Spiritual Values."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 20 Heresy I, Spirituality, commentary, p. 47; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberFourPredictions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Four predictions: chemistry, physics, biology and memory modification undo the human"@en, "Vier Vorhersagen: Chemie, Physik, Biologie und Gedächtnisveränderung lösen den Menschen auf"@de ;
    skos:definition "Chemistry undid personality, psychopharmacy showing that personality consists entirely of chemistry; physics undid determinacy, today not determining tomorrow; biology will soon undo most preconceptions about human beings, and if intelligent aliens have not shown up within a couple of decades we will probably have made our own; memory modification will undo the rest, memory defining identity. Chemicals can make one schizophrenic, ecstatic, depressed, libidinous or celibate: so much for the notion of essential self; we have awoken to chaos from Newton's sleep and single vision."@en ;
    skos:note "The third prediction carries a deadline, a couple of decades from a book whose author gives his age as forty-three; it is a prediction about the world and not a condition of failure of an operation, and is not counted as one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 21 Heresy II, Predictions, text, p. 48; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 21 Heresy II, Predictions, commentary, p. 49; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberSubmissionHeritage a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Our glorious simian heritage: submission to the bull ape behind every obeisance"@en, "Unser glorreiches Affenerbe: Unterwerfung unter den Leitaffen hinter jeder Ehrerbietung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Obeisance before the chieftain, penance to the shaman, genuflection to the Lord, confession to the priest and prostration before the almighty ape in the sky continue the offering of the arse to the bull ape of the troop; the expectation of reward and escape from punishment by submission lies deep in pack-animal brains and feeds the hunger for spiritual masters and deities, and endears the dog to the feeble-minded."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 22 Heresy III, Arse Licking, text, p. 50; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 22 Heresy III, Arse Licking, commentary, p. 51; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberAmplificationCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Magick amplifies whatever tendencies a person has, incompetence included"@en, "Magick verstärkt jede Neigung eines Menschen, auch die Unfähigkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Never give a wand to anyone who cannot handle ordinary reality. Magick will amplify whatever tendencies a person has, increasing general incompetence as readily as competence; the author has seen the well-organized make a magnificent success of their lives with magick and plenty of unpromising cases take a powered nose-dive to disaster with occult assistance. The best orders and books make the neophyte work very hard, for nothing of value comes from people who do not pursue excellence for its own sake; magick offers no escape from ordinary reality but a full-on confrontation with it, which one can easily lose."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: a powered nose-dive to disaster, an observable outcome of a life, put on the operator's prior incompetence."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberMagickDefined ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 23 Heresy IV, Elitism, text, p. 52; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 23 Heresy IV, Elitism, commentary, p. 53; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberHandlingOrdinaryReality a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The ability to handle ordinary reality"@en, "Die Fähigkeit, mit der gewöhnlichen Wirklichkeit umzugehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Competence in ordinary life before magick."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 23 Heresy IV, Elitism, text, p. 52; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberOrdinaryRealityBeforeWand a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Never give a wand to anyone who cannot handle ordinary reality"@en, "Nie jemandem einen Stab geben, der nicht mit der gewöhnlichen Wirklichkeit umgehen kann"@de ;
    skos:definition "To the Celtic saying never give a sword to a man who cannot dance the work adds: never give a wand to anyone who cannot handle ordinary reality, for magick amplifies incompetence as readily as competence."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberMagickDefined ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberHandlingOrdinaryReality ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Magick will increase general incompetence in life, and unpromising cases take a powered nose-dive to disaster with occult assistance." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 23 Heresy IV, Elitism, text, p. 52; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberOathOfTheAbyssRejected a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The Oath of the Abyss opens abysses to no constructive purpose"@en, "Der Eid des Abgrunds öffnet Abgründe ohne aufbauenden Zweck"@de ;
    skos:definition "The so-called Oath of the Abyss, in which the adept vows to interpret every phenomenon as a direct message from the ultimate reality to his or her self, will open several possible abysses but to no constructive purpose; the Golden Dawn and Thelemic teaching on the Abyss and its crossing is to be forgotten. Three major pitfalls await instead on the short and dangerous path of magick, and the initial exhilaration of accelerating into each may for a time mask mounting failure in reality."@en ;
    skos:note "Polemic against the Golden Dawn and Thelemic Abyss; no pm:Disputing is entered, since the work quotes no text of the other side."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 24 Heresy V, The Abysses, text, p. 54; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 24 Heresy V, The Abysses, commentary, p. 55; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberAbyssOfParanoia a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The Abyss of Paranoia: self-importance, then enemies fill the spaces reserved for them"@en, "Der Abgrund der Paranoia: Selbstwichtigkeit, dann füllen Feinde die ihnen vorbehaltenen Plätze"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Abyss of Paranoia pays initial dividends in self-importance, but as this effective paradigm takes hold things start to go wrong in a big way, and enemies will fill the spaces you reserve for them."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: things going wrong in a big way and enemies appearing, an observable outcome masked at first by exhilaration; put on the operator."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberMagickDefined ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 24 Heresy V, The Abysses, commentary, p. 55; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberAbyssOfSophistry a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The Abyss of Sophistry: the more effortlessly magickal one imagines oneself, the less effective"@en, "Der Abgrund der Sophisterei: je müheloser magisch man sich wähnt, desto weniger wirksam"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Abyss of Sophistry pays initial dividends in ease and leisure, but the more you imagine yourself innately and effortlessly magickal, the less effective you become."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: declining effectiveness while believing oneself effortlessly magickal; put on the operator."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberMagickDefined ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 24 Heresy V, The Abysses, commentary, p. 55; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberAbyssOfObsession a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The Abyss of Obsession: meaningfulness until the areas an insight misses explode"@en, "Der Abgrund der Besessenheit: Bedeutsamkeit, bis die von einer Einsicht verfehlten Bereiche explodieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Abyss of Obsession pays initial dividends in a hypertrophied feeling of meaningfulness; no insight can have universal application, and the areas it misses will eventually explode in your face."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the neglected areas of life exploding, observable but undated; put on the operator."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberMagickDefined ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 24 Heresy V, The Abysses, commentary, p. 55; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberDroppingTheWand a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Dropping the wand: effortless magick, True Will as excuse, declared enlightenment"@en, "Den Stab fallen lassen: mühelose Magick, Wahrer Wille als Ausrede, erklärte Erleuchtung"@de ;
    skos:definition "A checklist: does the power of the way you live cause magick to occur effortlessly; do you use True Will as an excuse to do nothing; do you think that when things go badly your inborn spiritual wisdom has arranged an initiation; do you no longer need your instruments and the technical procedures of spells and servitors; have you declared yourself enlightened? Either give up, tear off the adept's stripes and join the New-Agers, or pick up the wand and launch half a dozen tight sigils with full gnosis before dawn."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: five self-reported marks of having stopped practising, each a belief or omission the operator can check in himself; the remedy is countable, six sigils launched with gnosis before dawn."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberMagickDefined ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 25 Heresy VI, Dropping the Wand, text, p. 56; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberOffWhiteMagick a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Off-white magick between black and white magicians"@en, "Gebrochen-weiße Magick zwischen schwarzen und weißen Magiern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Self-professed black magicians seem universally unable to fight, fuck or buy their way out of wet paper bags despite fantasizing about becoming powerful psychopaths; self-professed white magicians spend their careers casting ineffectual spells at imaginary evils, and an averse pentagram scrawled on their doorstep as a joke can keep one busy for several years. Off-white magickians require no spiritual or demonic justification for their acts and can take your lover or money without leaving you feeling bad about it; Frater Stokastikos allied himselfs with the off-white tradition for its superior sense of humour."@en ;
    skos:note "The capacity to take another's lover or money by occult means is claimed for off-white magickians without an operation; entered in the definition and not as a yielding, since no practice is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 26 Heresy VII, Off-White Magick, text, p. 58; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberMagusChase a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magus chase: the magus issues the challenge Follow Me and retires as Ipsissimus"@en, "Die Magus-Jagd: der Magus fordert Folge mir und tritt als Ipsissimus zurück"@de ;
    skos:definition "Underneath any one-word paradigm such as Thelema or Chaos, a magickian assuming the role of magus within an order issues the challenge Follow Me; no stimulus to achievement matches a large body of people chasing you for blood, and no other motive for assuming the role has nobility. No honour attaches to those who will not retire when exhausted or who wait for their pursuers to lick their feet or crucify them. The retired magus may take the role of Ipsissimus, applying the treasures won in the race and shouting encouragement and criticism from the sidelines."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 27 Heresy VIII, Magus Chase, commentary, p. 61; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberAstrologyRejected a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Astrology has no place in magick"@en, "Astrologie hat in der Magick keinen Platz"@de ;
    skos:definition "The pseudoscience of astrology has no place in magick; it died once with the classical gods and again after the Enlightenment and resurfaced through the failure of contemporary psychology. One can make money with it easily and sometimes get people into bed with it, but otherwise forget it. The author once met an astrologer in a cast who had a perfectly rational reason for his broken leg."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 28 Heresy IX, Astrology, text, p. 62; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 28 Heresy IX, Astrology, commentary, p. 63; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberNewAgeism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "New-Ageism: a diluted enlightenment sold by old hippies"@en, "New-Age-Tum: eine verdünnte Erleuchtung, verkauft von alten Hippies"@de ;
    skos:definition "A poem lists what would make the New Age lovable: crystals that did something useful, astrology that told anything beyond the trite, corn circles from aliens rather than Wiltshire pranksters, channellers speaking hieroglyphics instead of pop-psychological twaddle. The hippies of 1968 broke the dress and sex codes, stopped a major war and created a healthy disrespect for authority, mysticism and music then functioning as enemies of the state; today's idealists have submerged their critical faculties under slop marketed by the same hippies. The poem also asks for dolphins as intelligent as cats that stopped rescuing sinking wood, whales that did something more impressive than occupy space, and a Goddess who made animals taste less good."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 29 Heresy X, New-Ageism, text, p. 64; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers", "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 29 Heresy X, New-Ageism, commentary, p. 65; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberGodOwnership a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "God ownership as a transmissible mental disease, dog ownership its minor form"@en, "Gottesbesitz als übertragbare Geisteskrankheit, Hundebesitz seine geringere Form"@de ;
    skos:definition "In domesticating the wolf we reduced the wild ideal to parodies of ourselfs, and so with our gods. In the transmissible mental disease of god ownership the victim believes that a being representing all his most desirable characteristics rules the entire universe; in the minor personality defect of dog ownership one seeks solace in a creature bred to show one's worst characteristics. Deus est Canis inversus."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 30 Heresy XI, Dog God, text, p. 66; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsyberAntichrist a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The papacy as the Antichrist: history's most grim and enduring conspiracy"@en, "Das Papsttum als Antichrist: die düsterste und dauerhafteste Verschwörung der Geschichte"@de ;
    skos:definition "Rome is called the heir of an empire that created a faith when its war machine failed. The Pauline Church is said to originate from a misogynist who never met the figure on whose myth he founded a cult, its doctrines rewritten at Nicaea as an instrument of the state; the papacy compromised its principles, burned and tortured opponents and allied itself with military adventures including fascism, its network of confessors and informers making it history's most grim and enduring conspiracy, now a parasite on its host, at a cost put at a hundred million killed and two centuries of history."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PsyberMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, PsyberMagick (New Falcon Publications, ISBN 1-56184-092-0), ch. 31 Heresy XII, The Antichrist, text, p. 68; read in a full-text layer with printed page numbers" .

